                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_51\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit adds a single `break;` statement right after the \"Cover downloaded for ...\" debug log line inside the loop over candidate cover URLs in `CoverManager.getCoverBytes()`. Without this `break`, once a cover is successfully downloaded/read, the loop keeps iterating over the remaining URLs for that same retriever, which can overwrite the already-successfully-fetched `coverBytes` with a later (possibly null or wrong) result \u2014 this is the \"local cover art\" bug described in the fix message.\n\nTracing the history of this exact method:\n- candidate_04 is the commit that originally introduced `CoverManager.java` (and the `getCoverBytes(String[] coverUrls, CoverInfo coverInfo)` method). At that point the loop body read:\n  ```\n  if (coverBytes != null) {\n      ...\n      return coverBytes;\n  }\n  ```\n  The `return coverBytes;` here functioned exactly like a `break` \u2014 it immediately exited the loop (and the method) as soon as a valid cover was found, so the bug did not exist yet.\n\n- candidate_51 refactored this same method (and sibling methods like `executeGetRequest`/`cleanGetRequest`/`isWifi`) to a \"single exit point\" style, pre-declaring the return variable and removing early `return`s in favor of falling through to one `return` at the end of the method. In doing so, it deleted the `return coverBytes;` line from inside the `if (coverBytes != null) { ... }` block but did NOT add a `break;` in its place. This is the exact diff:\n  ```\n  d(CoverManager.class.getSimpleName(),\n          \"Cover downloaded for \" + coverInfo.getAlbum() + \" from \" + url\n                  + \", size=\" + coverBytes.length);\n  -                    return coverBytes;\n  }\n  ```\n  This change silently converted the early-exit into a no-op fall-through, so the loop continued to the next URL even after a cover was already found \u2014 introducing the local-cover-art overwrite bug.\n\n- candidate_52 (a later, unrelated-looking commit) coincidentally touches the very same lines and its diff is textually identical to the fix commit's diff (adding `break;` back at this location, same line numbers and same before/after hashes as the fix). This indicates candidate_52 is effectively the fix commit itself (or an equivalent re-application of it), not a bug-introducing candidate \u2014 selecting it would be circular.\n\nGiven that candidate_04 had correct (return-based) early-exit behavior and candidate_51 is the commit that removed that early exit without substituting a `break`, candidate_51 is the commit that introduced the bug fixed by the target commit.\n"
                }
              }
